> > What's a dwimmer?

> From DWIM, "Do What I Mean." It's a software feature (typically part
> of some command-line interface) which, when the user gives a
> non-sensical command, attempts to guess what they really meant and do
> that instead.

> The few dwimmers that've actually gotten implemented have been notable
> chiefly for their ability to not only guess wrong, but to somehow pick
> the most damaging of the plausible wrong options.

I didn't know what a dwimmer was yesterday, and in looking up info
about it I discovered the DND term "dweomer," which is a sort of
powerful magic with a sentient (usually malevolent) will of its own,
which inhabits an object and is bound to it.

Couldn't help but notice the similarity between this
resident-and-usually-evil magic and a potentially-very-evil
mis-interpreter of manually entered strings. And a (very shallow)
search reveals no etymology for the DND term. Coincidence, or did the
DND guys pick it up from hacker lore?

(disclaimer: I am not a DND player, much less GM and I probably don't
really grok it, but that's my impression of the term at least.)



